    WEEK 12 / ESSAY ARCHIVE
  </p>

  <h3>A HERO'S JOURNEY</h3>

  <p>
    Adventure. Saving the world from an evil force against all odds, usually with a ragtag group of companions. Throw in a romance subplot and you’ve got a classic: <em>Star Wars</em>, <em>The Lord of the Rings</em>. Luke Skywalker and Frodo Baggins are true heroes, possessing grit, bravery, strength, and intelligence. Their stories follow the hero’s journey: a series of specific steps that the hero must take to achieve their goal or complete their quest. Outside of books, television, and movies, we each go on our own hero’s journey. Allegedly. My “hero’s journey” consists of three DUIs, addiction, rehab, and incarceration. Not exactly the same résumé as our fictional heroes and definitely not the same as great people of history. I am not a hero, but we each have a hero’s journey, so that apparently makes me a hero. My experiences aren’t some noble quest to save the kingdom—it’s a journey to save myself.
  </p>

  <p>
    The hero’s journey typically involves the protagonist confronting extraordinary challenges, but my experiences raise a different question. Many of my challenges have been consequences of my own decisions. I was in jail because of choices I made, not because I volunteered to fight an evil wizard. I am just a protagonist, someone whose story is followed, and protagonists can be wrong—Walter White, BoJack Horseman, Michael Corleone. Although the hero’s journey presents adversity as a path toward transformation and wisdom, surviving hardship doesn’t automatically make someone a hero or a better person. My experiences with addiction, DUIs, incarceration, and sobriety have led to personal and spiritual growth, but that growth came through accountability, self-reflection, and deliberate effort—not simply because I suffered. Unlike the heroes in stories, my transformation doesn't have a triumphant ending; it's an ongoing process shaped by the choices I continue to make.
  </p>

  <p>
    The first step of the hero’s journey is the call to adventure. That’s if you’re a hero. I’m just me—a protagonist. My first part of the hero’s journey is when I got my first DUI and initially recognized that my relationship with alcohol might need to change. Thinking it was an isolated incident, and given the fact that it was 2020 and during COVID, I used that as an excuse to keep drinking due to “stress” during the pandemic. I was also suffering from severe sciatica pain, and I invented another excuse to continue consuming alcohol. I wasn’t called to an adventure; I was called to a courtroom.
  </p>

  <p>
    I obviously ignored the lesson of the first step, which brings us to the second phase of the hero’s journey: refusal of the call. I continued to drink despite the consequences, and this is why addiction complicates the idea of simply choosing change. I continued to delude myself into thinking I didn’t have a problem, and I thought I could keep it under control—another lie I told myself. I saw no reason to change, and I saw no reason to stop entirely. Holding the belief that I was just fine, I continued living life drinking daily and going to bars almost every day as well. I struggled to change and am responsible for my behavior and continuing to drink, but addiction involves more than a simple decision to just stop; it’s not that simple. I began to experience bipolar symptoms around this time, and I tried to drink them away. While experiencing this adversity, transformation was not happening automatically. My life, habits, and mind were not changing simply because I was going through a tough time. The consequences of my DUI didn’t necessarily lead me to sobriety because I was still drinking during this time.
  </p>

  <p>
    Next is meeting the mentor. After I had my first psychotic episode in December 2020, I found a therapist I really clicked with. She became my therapist for four years. She helped me understand a lot about myself and bipolar disorder, how to live with it and how to cope, what living with bipolar disorder looked like, and what I could expect going forward, as well as working on other issues. I took all of her guidance to heart and grew a lot. We met weekly, and I began to heal. While in jail, I met the jail chaplain, and we discussed spirituality in depth. He gave me many spiritual texts to read. My favorite was the <em>Dhammapada</em>, a Buddhist text. I read it cover to cover many times and began to practice the wisdom in its pages. The people teaching me could only teach me—it was up to me to put it into practice.
  </p>

  <p>
    The fourth stage of the hero’s journey is crossing the threshold. This is when I went to jail. In traditional stories, this is when the hero leaves the familiar world and enters an unfamiliar one. Frodo leaves the Shire. Luke Skywalker leaves Tatooine. …and I went to jail. The similarity between my path and the true hero’s journey is that the protagonist’s familiar life is disrupted. For me, incarceration meant losing my freedom and being shut indoors, in a nutshell. I lost total control over my life, was away from everyone I loved, entered a dangerous environment, and had to adjust to a completely new way of living.
  </p>

  <p>
    Next is tests, allies, and enemies. In traditional stories, the protagonist encounters obstacles, makes friends, confronts enemies, and develops the skills for the challenges ahead. I had a lot of obstacles in jail. One was isolation and feeling forgotten. Others were loss of independence, uncertainty, and being in my own thoughts. I had little control over my daily circumstances, was living in a restricted environment, and had assumptions, fears, and difficult emotions that plagued me daily. I wrote in my jail journal, “Hell, nobody will even notice I’m gone.” Allies I had in jail were books—I read 52 in five months—and I had my spirituality, the jail chaplain, a few friends I made, and my journal. My enemies were my thinking patterns and the things that got me into jail in the first place: avoidance, impulsivity, not speaking up when I was struggling, and distorted thinking. I experienced post-traumatic growth in jail by developing self-compassion, improving relationships, and learning to accept help. I learned from others and from myself and implemented those changes through work, not just because I was behind bars.
  </p>

  <p>
    In the hero’s journey, we’re at the approach to the inmost cave stage. In traditional stories, this is when the hero approaches the most dangerous or frightening part of their adventure. They're preparing to confront something that could fundamentally change them. In my story, this is when I went to rehab. I had to face addiction, look critically at the consequences of my DUIs, and examine my relationship with alcohol. I discussed very personal stories, experiences, and behaviors with therapists and the women in treatment with me while I was developing the knowledge and practical tools to maintain sobriety when I returned to everyday life. Jail restricted my freedom, but rehab represented a place where I could begin preparing to exercise that freedom differently.
  </p>

  <p>
    The ordeal comes next. Frodo must carry the Ring into Mordor. Luke Skywalker has to confront Darth Vader. For me, it was leaving rehab and returning to everyday life. In treatment, I learned about addiction, identified patterns, developed coping mechanisms, and prepared to live differently. Eventually, I had to take everything I had learned and apply it outside that environment. And unlike Frodo, I can’t just throw my addiction into the fires of Mount Doom and watch it disappear. In rehab, I learned a lot of skills about how to cope outside of those walls with cravings, urges, temptations, situations involving substances, and my own scar tissue. I was eager to return home, begin this sobriety handbook project, and count the days I have been sober. I felt very prepared by rehab to come home. I was taking responsibility for my past at this point and taking responsibility for my future. My DUIs and incarceration are a part of my past, but the choices I make and my sobriety are part of my future that I am choosing to continue. I acknowledge that addiction complicates decision-making, but that doesn’t eliminate accountability.
  </p>

  <p>
    Continuing on, we arrive at the reward. In traditional stories, the hero survives the ordeal and receives something valuable: treasure, wisdom, freedom, a magical object, or a newfound understanding of themselves. I have gained a lot, but it’s not from DUIs, trauma, incarceration, or going to rehab. It’s from my own work. I’ve gained spirituality from prayer, meditation, and studying different spiritual traditions. I’ve found inner peace through acceptance, mindfulness, and radical acceptance I developed while incarcerated and practiced afterward. Self-understanding was found through hard work; I gained a better understanding of my behavior, emotional responses, and personal values by simply observing myself. My relationships and appreciation for my loved ones have grown immensely. My independence has grown. My creativity has improved and is flying high. I’ve gained so much not just by living through things, but by cultivating skills, learning, and working on myself during those times.
  </p>

  <p>
    We’re at the road back. For me, this means returning to everyday life after rehab and learning how to live with the changes I’ve made. Traditional heroes often return home transformed, bringing new knowledge or abilities into familiar surroundings. But recovery doesn’t necessarily mean becoming the person you were before addiction. I wasn’t necessarily returning to an old identity; I was continuing to develop my existing one. I didn’t return home to a big celebration either—just a few “welcome homes” from friends, family, and neighbors.
  </p>

  <p>
    The next stage is resurrection. It represents a symbolic death and rebirth where the protagonist undergoes a significant transformation. I don’t necessarily believe I became a significantly different person through sobriety, which makes this stage worth examining. I wasn’t a fundamentally different person after incarceration and rehab, but I needed major growth. Major parts of my personality, interests, and values remained unchanged, but my beliefs and my relationship with myself and others drastically changed. My morals changed. I grew up. The way I made decisions and the way I thought changed. Recovery required abandoning former parts of my identity and my beliefs, but it also involved changing my behavior while retaining who I am. There is a distinction between reinvention and growth, and I believe I did a little of both. I reinvented the way I think and my beliefs around spirituality and my relationships with others, but I remained who I am. I didn’t emerge from rehab or from jail an enlightened new person—just a better version of myself.
  </p>

  <p>
    The return is next—where the hero returns to the ordinary world with newfound wisdom, knowledge, or something valuable that benefits others. Frodo helps save Middle-earth. Luke Skywalker helps restore freedom to the galaxy. I have my experiences, my recovery, and the knowledge I’ve gained from both. I returned home with perspectives I developed through my experiences with addiction, incarceration, therapy, and sobriety. My understanding of addiction changed how I respond to other people experiencing it with empathy: I can fully “get it.” I know what it’s like. I can speak the language, know what they’re going through, understand the habits, and fully recognize what an addict experiences. I have empathy for these individuals. I also approach relationships differently. I have also become more open about my experiences and what I’ve done, gone through, and what my life looks like, just in case someone out there needs to know that they aren’t alone. I am a bipolar addict who has been to jail, has three DUIs, is spiritual, has been to rehab, and is in recovery. Those are just facets of who I am. I’m not special, but I can offer perspectives.
  </p>

  <p>
    Finally, we are at the last stage: the freedom to live. This is when the hero reaches a state of freedom, enlightenment, or harmony after completing the quest. The villain has been defeated, the kingdom has been saved, and everyone lives happily ever after. Except recovery doesn’t come with closing credits. I’ve experienced the literal loss of freedom when I was in jail, and freedom isn’t necessarily just being able to leave my house or make my own decisions. Freedom to me is being free of the chains of addiction. Freedom from alcohol for me means so much more than waking up without a hangover. It means being happy, being happy in relationships, making others and myself proud, accomplishing my goals, living in my spirituality, loving myself, and so much more. When I was circling the drain and drinking, all I cared about was alcohol and getting the next drink. Now, I care about my life. That is true freedom. My recovery doesn’t erase my past, the things I did, or the consequences of my actions, but it does shape my future. And this isn’t the final version of myself—I’m still growing and learning.
  </p>

  <p>
    And I’m no hero. Luke Skywalker saved the galaxy. Frodo Baggins saved Middle-earth. I went to jail, I went to rehab, I drank. What I did was grow, learn about myself, and put down a beer and a bottle. I became a better person and saved myself. Not sure if that makes me a hero, but I saved myself.
  </p>

